          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"2-2\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_1\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"preserved\",\"summary\":\"The main business activities are largely represented with acceptable paraphrase and granularity, including data entry/checking, contract-document retry, grid-operator handling, contract acceptance/withdrawal, and meter-data import.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Most core precedence is preserved, but the final join adds an unsupported order: customer-service import of meter data can occur only after the old-supplier billing branch completes, whereas the source allows import after customer service receives the meter data.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The late-stage parallel section is synchronized incorrectly: customer-service continuation is forced to wait for unrelated old-supplier/grid-operator work. There is also a possible over-continuation from the supplier-concurrence path back into CIS contract creation, though that part is textually ambiguous.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"wrong_parallelism_or_synchronization\",\"explanation\":\"The source says that after the grid operator sends meter data to customer service, customer service imports that data into its systems, while the grid operator/old-supplier billing activities occur alongside and end for those parties. In the generated graph, the forked billing branch and the customer-service meter-data branch are joined before import, so importing meter data wrongly depends on completion of old-supplier billing work. This is a real synchronization/ordering change, but it is confined to the final part of an otherwise largely faithful process model, so the whole-graph impact is moderate rather than fundamental. Separately, the text is unclear about whether the supplier-concurrence branch should later rejoin before CIS contract creation; the graph does rejoin it, which may or may not be an additional defect.\",\"overall_score\":0.8,\"severity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"ambiguity_flag\":true,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"The source clearly states CIS contract creation for the no-objection/no-supplier-concurrence case, but it is not fully explicit whether the supplier-concurrence branch, after conflict handling, should eventually continue to the same CIS-contract step. That interpretive difference could change whether the graph has an additional control-flow defect and could affect the score.\",\"requires_human_review\":true,\"review_reason\":\"Human review is warranted because the supplier-concurrence continuation is materially ambiguous in the source and could change the final diagnosis or score.\"}"
        }
      ],
